{
  "title": "Glenohumeral Internal Rotation Deficit (GIRD)",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/shoulder-and-elbow/3055/glenohumeral-internal-rotation-deficit-gird",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Mar 10 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Glenohumeral internal rotation deficit (GIRD) is a condition resulting in the loss of internal rotation of the glenohumeral joint as compared to the contralateral shoulder, most commonly seen in the throwing athlete.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with a decrease in internal rotation, increase in external rotation, with a decrease in total arc of rotation compared to the contralateral shoulder.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment consists of physical therapy with a focus on posteroinferior capsular stretching.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs primarily in overhead athletes",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "often seen in baseball pitchers",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "caused by repetitive throwing",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "thought to occur during the late cocking and early acceleration phase",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "tightening of posterior capsule or posteroinferior capsule leads to translation of humeral head (**capsular constraint mechanism)**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "translation of humeral head is in the OPPOSITE direction from area of capsular tightening",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "posterior capsular tightness leads to anterosuperior translation of humeral head in flexion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/antsup.jpg",
                      "caption": "Anterosuperior translation (because of posterior capsular tightness)"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "posterorinferior capsular tightness leads to **posterosuperior** translation of humeral head in ABER",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/peelback.jpg",
                      "caption": "Peel back"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "anterior capsule is stretched",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**glenohumeral instability**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**internal impingement**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "abutment of the greater tuberosity against the posterosuperior glenoid during abduction and external rotation leads to pinching of posterosuperior rotator cuff",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/Illustration_moved.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**articular-sided partial rotator cuff tears**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "tensile failure in excessive rotation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "internal impingement",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**SLAP lesion**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "throwers with GIRD are 25% more likely to have a SLAP lesion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "peel-back mechanism (biceps anchor and postero superior labrum peels back) during late cocking",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "because of posterosuperior translation of humeral head and change in biceps vector force posteriorly",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/peelback.jpg",
                          "caption": "Peel back"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Glenohumeral joint",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "vague shoulder pain",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "sometimes painless",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may report a decrease in throwing performance",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**stabilize the scapula** to obtain true measure of glenohumeral rotation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "increased **sulcus sign**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "due to stretching of anterior structures that resist external rotation (coracohumeral ligament, rotator interval)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "characterized by altered glenohumeral range of motion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "decrease in internal rotation and increase in external rotation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "if the GIRD (loss of internal rotation) is less than external rotation gain (ERG), the shoulder maintains normal kinematics",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if the GIRD exceeds external rotation gain (ERG), this leads to deranged kinematics",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "decrease in internal rotation is usually greater than a 25° difference as compared to non-throwing shoulder",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recommended views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "AP and lateral of glenohumeral joint",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "usually normal",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "may show increased **glenoid retroversion**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/retroverted%20glenoid.jpg",
                  "caption": "radiograph showing a retroverted glenoid."
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**MRI**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**ABER view on MRI can show associated lesions**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/aber.005.jpg",
                  "caption": "shows technique for ABER view with MRI"
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/mri%20labral%20tear.jpg",
                  "caption": "shows ABER view of the GH joint"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**rest from throwing and physical therapy for 6 months**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "first line of treatment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "physical therapy",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "posteroinferior capsule stretching",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "**sleeper stretch**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/sleeper%20stretch_moved.jpg",
                              "caption": ""
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": [
                            {
                              "text": "performed with internal rotation stretch at 90 degrees abduction with scapular stabilization",
                              "table": null,
                              "images": [],
                              "importance": "5",
                              "children": []
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "roll-over sleeper stretch",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/rollover.jpg",
                              "caption": "Rollover sleeper stretch"
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": [
                            {
                              "text": "arm flexed 60° and body rolled forward 30°",
                              "table": null,
                              "images": [],
                              "importance": "1",
                              "children": []
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "doorway stretch",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/doorway.jpg",
                              "caption": "Doorway stretch"
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "cross-body adduction stretch",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/crossbody.jpg",
                              "caption": "Cross body adduction stretch"
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**pectoralis minor stretching**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "rotator cuff and periscapular strengthening",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "90% of young throwers respond to sleeper stretches/PT",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "10% of older throwers do not respond, and will need arthroscopic release eventually",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "posteroinferior capsule release vs. anterior stabilization",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "only indicated if extensive PT fails",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Techniques",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Posterior capsule release vs. anterior stabilization**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3055/images/capsular_release.jpg",
              "caption": "Arthroscopic capsular release"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "some advocate posterior capsule release while others advocate anterior stabilization",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "repair thinned rotator cuff if significantly thinned (transcuff or takedown and repair)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "technique controversial",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "for throwing athlete with posteroinferior capsular contracture, release posterior inferior capsule and posterior band of IGHL",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "electrocautery inserted through posterior portal, camera from anterior portal",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "from 9 to 6 o'clock position",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "at level of glenoid rim",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "until rotator cuff fibers (behind the capsule) can be seen from within joint",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "insert arthroscopic shaver to widen gap in capsule (prevents recurrence)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "gentle manipulation at the end completes release of any remaining fibers, maximizes IR and flexion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "results",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "will immediately gain 65° of internal rotation postop",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": []
